%S A400607 6,6,8,9,10,18,20,24,24,25,25,28,30,32,35,36,45,50,50,50,50,54,54,63,
%T A400607 64,70,70,72,77,80,81,96,96,98,112,120,121,125,126,126,128,140,150,
%U A400607 150,162,175,176,180,200,216,216,221,231,242,243,243,245,252,270,280,280
%N A400607 Integers p occurring in solutions of m^2 = p! * q! * r!, with p > q > r > 1, ordered by increasing m (A400208).
%C A400607 This sequence gives the corresponding values of p for the solutions to Problem 1, Round Two, of the 2023 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad, which asks whether there exist infinitely many triples of integers p > q > r > 1 such that p! * q! * r! is a perfect square m^2.
%C A400607 Terms a(1) = 3! and a(9) = 4! come from the family (m, p, q, r) = ((t!)!, t!, t!-1, t), t >= 3. Here t = 3 and t = 4, respectively.
%C A400607 The family (p, q, r) = (k! * x^2, k! * x^2 - 1, k), k > 1, x >= 1, gives also infinitely many solutions.
%C A400607 -> For r = 2, taking x = 2, 3, 4, 5 gives a(3) = 8, a(6) = 18, a(14) = 32, a(21) = 50.
%C A400607 -> For r = 3, taking x = 1, 2 gives a(2) = 6 and a(8) = 24.
%H A400607 New Zealand Mathematical Olympiad Committee, <a href="https://www.mathsolympiad.org.nz/competitions/nzmo/files/problems.php?round=2&amp;year=2023&amp;type=solutions">2023 NZMO Round 2, problem 1</a>.
%H A400607 <a href="/index/O#Olympiads">Index to sequences related to Olympiads and other Mathematical Competitions</a>.
%e A400607 For a(5) = 10, we have 10! * 7! * 6! = 3628800^2.
%o A400607 (PARI) upto(n) = {res = List(); for(i = 6, n, process(i)); res}
%o A400607 process(n) = {if(isprime(n), return); minq = precprime(n-1); nf = n!; for(q = minq, n-1, qf = q!; for(r = 2, q-1, if(issquare(qf * r! * n!),print1(n", "); listput(res, n))))} \\ _David A. Corneth_, Oct 01 2026
%Y A400607 Cf. A400208.
%K A400607 nonn,new
%O A400607 1,1
